## Problem

The inflation ratio is tracked as arch_out <= comp_in * 128 at archive-frame scope (budget.rs line 425-426). A crafted archive with 99 stored (1:1) entries accumulates compressed-input credit that a subsequent bomb entry can exploit. The per-entry byte cap (64 MiB) catches this only when configured lower than accumulated_comp_in * ratio.

## Why This Matters

The DEFLATE algorithm has a theoretical maximum ratio of 1032:1 (zlib.net/zlib_tech.html). David Fifield's zip bomb research (WOOT'19) demonstrates that structural tricks (overlapping entries) can achieve 28 million:1 effective ratios. Per-entry ratio tracking closes the gap between honest archive with one suspicious entry and entire archive is suspicious.

How other scanners handle this:
- ClamAV: Tracks per-scan-root decompression totals. Uses MaxScanSize (400 MB default) as absolute cap.
- TruffleHog: Per-file size limits (2 GB) + per-archive depth (10) + timeout (60s). No ratio tracking.
- Go archive/zip: Post-decompression check if r.nread > r.f.UncompressedSize64 — per-entry only.

scanner-rs is already ahead of these with frame-level ratio tracking. Per-entry ratio is the missing piece.

## Files to Modify

- src/archive/budget.rs: Add entry_compressed_in: u64 to ArchiveFrame, reset in begin_entry_scan, compute rem_entry_ratio in charge_decompressed_out()
- src/archive/outcome.rs: New variant EntrySkipReason::InflationRatioExceeded = 9

